{% set a = s.attribution %}{% set b = s.bottom %}{% set v = s.validation %}
{% set DIM = {'short_delivery': 'Short deliveries', 'late_delivery': 'Late deliveries', 'quality_rejection': 'Poor quality', 'price_premium': 'High prices'} %}
{% set bottom_rows = suppliers | selectattr('supplier_id', 'in', b.suppliers) | list %}
{% set names = bottom_rows | map(attribute='supplier_name') | list %}
{% set worst = bottom_rows[0] %}
{% set rule = a.baselines.most_recent_batch %}

<div class="meta"><span>MCCIA Applied AI Studio · Take-home assignment</span><span>Approach, solution and assumptions</span></div>

<h1>Supplier Blindspot</h1>
<p class="lede">Which suppliers are quietly costing Arora Traders money, how much, and what to say to them. This document explains how the answer was found, in plain words, and what was assumed along the way.</p>
<div class="links"><span><b>Live app</b> <a href="{{ cfg.LIVE_URL }}">{{ cfg.LIVE_URL | replace('https://', '') }}</a></span><span><b>Source code</b> <a href="{{ cfg.REPO_URL }}">{{ cfg.REPO_URL | replace('https://', '') }}</a></span></div>

<div class="summary">
  <h3>The answer in one minute</h3>
  <ol>
    <li><b>{{ ['No', 'One', 'Two', 'Three', 'Four', 'Five'][names|length] if names|length < 6 else names|length }} suppliers are the problem: {{ names | join(', ') }}.</b> They get only {{ b.share_of_spend | pct(0) }} of the orders but cause {{ b.share_of_impact | pct(0) }} of all the money lost, <b>{{ b.total_impact | lakh }} over {{ '%.0f'|format(s.period.years) }} years</b>.</li>
    <li><b>The money is lost in plain ways:</b> material that was billed but never arrived, material customers sent back, and material rejected at Arora’s own gate. Every rupee is worked out order by order and can be checked.</li>
    <li><b>{{ s.counts.returns_blank }} of the {{ s.counts.returns }} customer returns had no supplier written down.</b> A model traces each one to its most likely supplier. When tested on returns where the answer was known, it put only {{ a.metrics.misallocation | pct }} of the value on the wrong supplier, against {{ rule.misallocation | pct(0) }} for the rule suggested in the assignment.</li>
    {% if v.available and v.flagged %}<li><b>The result was double-checked.</b> The dataset secretly marks which suppliers are bad. That column was locked away and never used; opened at the end, it named the same {{ v.hits | length }} of {{ v.flagged | length }} suppliers.</li>{% endif %}
    <li><b>Each bad supplier gets a ready-to-use negotiation brief</b>, with the money they owe, the orders that prove it and what they are likely to argue back. The recommendation is to replace them, and existing suppliers who can take over each material are named.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="kpis">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Lost to the bottom {{ names|length }}</div><div class="v red">{{ b.total_impact | lakh }}</div><div class="s">over {{ '%.0f'|format(s.period.years) }} years, from {{ b.share_of_spend | pct(0) }} of the orders</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Lost across all suppliers</div><div class="v">{{ s.totals.total_impact | lakh }}</div><div class="s">{{ s.counts.suppliers }} suppliers, {{ "{:,}".format(s.counts.orders) }} orders</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="l">Untraced returns</div><div class="v">{{ s.counts.returns_blank }} of {{ s.counts.returns }}</div><div class="s">traced to their likely supplier</div></div>
  {% if v.available and v.flagged %}<div class="kpi"><div class="l">Double-check</div><div class="v green">{{ v.hits | length }} of {{ v.flagged | length }}</div><div class="s">match the dataset’s hidden answer</div></div>
  {% else %}<div class="kpi"><div class="l">Double-check</div><div class="v">n/a</div><div class="s">no hidden answer in this data</div></div>{% endif %}
</div>

<h2><span class="no">1</span>The problem, in plain words</h2>
<p>Arora Traders buys steel from {{ s.counts.suppliers }} suppliers. Some deliver less than they bill for, some deliver late, and some deliver material that fails inspection or comes back from customers. Nobody had added this up per supplier, and {{ (s.counts.returns_blank / s.counts.returns) | pct(0) }} of the customer returns don’t even say which supplier the material came from. That is the “blindspot”.</p>
<p>The data covers {{ s.period.start }} to {{ s.period.end }}: {{ "{:,}".format(s.counts.orders) }} purchase orders, their deliveries and payments, {{ s.counts.returns }} customer returns, a market price list and a supplier list. The assignment asked for five things, and each has a clear home:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>What was asked</th><th class="n">Marks</th><th>Where to find it</th></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Money lost per supplier</b>, in rupees</td><td class="n">40%</td><td>Section 3; every supplier’s page in the app</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Trace returns</b> that have no supplier recorded</td><td class="n">35%</td><td>Section 4; the app’s “Untraced returns” page</td></tr>
  <tr><td><b>Negotiation briefs</b> for the worst suppliers</td><td class="n">25%</td><td>Section 7; the app’s “Briefs” page (printable PDFs)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Clean the data; build a <b>scorecard</b>; recommend <b>who to replace</b></td><td class="n">—</td><td>Sections 2, 5 and 7; the app’s Overview</td></tr>
</table>

<h2><span class="no">2</span>How it works, in five steps</h2>
<p>One command (<code>python -m pipeline.run</code>) takes the six raw files and produces everything: the scorecard, the rupee figures, the traced returns, the briefs, the website’s data and this document. The same steps run in the cloud when someone uploads their own files on the website.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="step"><div class="k">STEP 1</div><div class="t">Read and clean</div><div class="d">Recognise each file by its columns, fix Indian number and date formats, and stop loudly if anything doesn’t add up.</div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="k">STEP 2</div><div class="t">Trace the returns</div><div class="d">Work out the most likely supplier for each return with no supplier recorded.</div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="k">STEP 3</div><div class="t">Count the money</div><div class="d">Put a rupee figure on every short delivery, return and rejection, order by order.</div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="k">STEP 4</div><div class="t">Score and rank</div><div class="d">Score each supplier out of 100 on four checks and rank all {{ s.counts.suppliers }}.</div></div>
  <div class="step"><div class="k">STEP 5</div><div class="t">Write the briefs</div><div class="d">Turn the evidence into a two-page brief for each of the worst suppliers.</div></div>
</div>

<h3>What cleaning the data turned up</h3>
<p>Before any scoring, every assumption the arithmetic depends on was tested. A few findings shaped the whole approach:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>The answer was hidden in the data.</b> The supplier list has a column marking the bad suppliers. Using it would be cheating, so it is removed the moment the files are read and only opened at the very end, as a check (section 6).</li>
  <li><b>Suppliers bill for what was ordered, not what arrived.</b> This is true on every order, so the gap between what was billed and what arrived is money paid for nothing.</li>
  <li><b>The market price list can’t judge suppliers.</b> It covers only {{ s.index_context | length }} of the {{ s.counts.materials }} materials, and everyone pays well above it. Prices are therefore compared with what <i>other suppliers</i> charged for the same material in the same quarter.</li>
  <li><b>Some fields contradict each other</b>, for example “days late” in payments doesn’t match its own dates. These are recalculated from the raw dates, and every fix is logged ({{ quality.findings | length }} findings in total).</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="no">3</span>Counting the money lost <span class="muted small">· 40% of marks</span></h2>
<p>Money is counted per purchase order and then added up per supplier. The assignment’s own formula is used exactly as given:</p>
<div class="box">
  <b>Money lost = short delivery + customer returns</b><br>
  <b>Short delivery</b> = what was billed − (what actually arrived × the agreed price)<br>
  <b>Customer returns</b> = tonnes returned × the supplier’s usual price for that material<br>
  <span class="muted small">For a return with no supplier recorded, the value is shared out by the model’s probabilities (section 4), so every returned tonne is counted exactly once.</span>
</div>
<p>Two further losses are real but outside that formula, so they are <b>shown separately</b> and never mixed into the assignment’s figure: <b>material rejected at Arora’s gate</b> (rejected tonnes × price) and the <b>cost of handling a return</b> (an assumed {{ (cfg.RETURN_HANDLING_FACTOR*100)|round|int }}% of its value).</p>

<h3>Worked example: {{ worst.supplier_name }}</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Where the money went</th><th class="n">Amount</th><th>How it was worked out</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Billed but never delivered</td><td class="n">{{ worst.short_loss | inr }}</td><td>{{ '%.1f'|format(worst.qty_short) }} tonnes short across {{ worst.orders }} orders</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Returned by customers</td><td class="n">{{ worst.return_loss | inr }}</td><td>{{ worst.returns_recorded|int }} returns on record, plus about {{ worst.returns_inferred|round|int }} untraced returns that are most likely theirs</td></tr>
  <tr class="total"><td>The assignment’s formula</td><td class="n">{{ worst.rubric_core_total | inr }}</td><td></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rejected at our gate</td><td class="n">{{ worst.reject_loss | inr }}</td><td>{{ '%.1f'|format(worst.qty_rejected) }} tonnes failed inspection</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Handling the returns</td><td class="n">{{ worst.handling_loss | inr }}</td><td>{{ (cfg.RETURN_HANDLING_FACTOR*100)|round|int }}% of return value (assumed)</td></tr>
  <tr class="total"><td>Total</td><td class="n">{{ worst.total_impact | inr }}</td><td>{{ '%.1f'|format(worst.impact_pct_of_spend) }}% of everything bought from them</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>The worst suppliers against everyone else</h3>
<div class="bars">
  <div class="bar"><span>Share of orders</span><div class="track"><div class="fill dark" style="width: {{ (b.share_of_spend*100)|round(1) }}%"></div></div><span class="v">{{ b.share_of_spend | pct(0) }}</span></div>
  <div class="bar"><span>Share of money lost</span><div class="track"><div class="fill red" style="width: {{ (b.share_of_impact*100)|round(1) }}%"></div></div><span class="v">{{ b.share_of_impact | pct(0) }}</span></div>
</div>
<table>
  <tr><th>Supplier</th><th class="n">Rank</th><th class="n">Short delivery</th><th class="n">Returns</th><th class="n">Formula total</th><th class="n">Everything</th></tr>
  {% for r in suppliers[:6] %}
  <tr class="{{ 'hl' if r.supplier_id in b.suppliers else '' }}"><td>{{ r.supplier_name }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.rank }} of {{ s.counts.suppliers }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.short_loss | lakh }}</td><td class="n">{{ r.return_loss | lakh }}</td><td class="n"><b>{{ r.rubric_core_total | lakh }}</b></td><td class="n">{{ r.total_impact | lakh }}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
  <tr class="total"><td colspan="4">All {{ s.counts.suppliers }} suppliers</td><td class="n">{{ s.totals.rubric_core_total | lakh }}</td><td class="n">{{ s.totals.total_impact | lakh }}</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Does it match the assignment’s own estimate?</b> The assignment guesses the three bad suppliers cost <b>₹8–12 lakh a year</b> on ₹70–85 lakh of monthly buying. The data actually shows far more buying ({{ s.counts.monthly_spend | lakh }} a month), so the raw totals are bigger. Scaled back to the assignment’s buying level, the worst suppliers lose <b>{{ b.scaled_to_ps_low | lakh }} – {{ b.scaled_to_ps_high | lakh }} a year</b> more than an average supplier would, which is inside the assignment’s range. It was reached independently, not tuned to fit.</p>

<h2><span class="no">4</span>Tracing returns with no supplier <span class="muted small">· 35% of marks</span></h2>
<p>{{ s.counts.returns_blank }} customer returns say what came back, when and why, but not which supplier sent it. They can’t simply be ignored, because that would let the worst suppliers off the hook for a large share of their returns.</p>
<h3>The idea</h3>
<p>Think of it like narrowing down a suspect list. For each untraced return, the <b>suspects</b> are the suppliers who had delivered that material before the return date. For each suspect, the model looks at clues that were known on that date, never later:</p>
<ul>{% for k, label in a.feature_labels.items() %}<li>{{ label }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
<p>It learns how much each clue matters from the {{ a.metrics.n }} returns where the supplier <i>is</i> recorded, then gives every suspect a probability. The return’s value is shared out by those probabilities rather than pinned on one supplier.</p>

<h3>Does it work? Tested with the answer hidden</h3>
<p>The model was tested on the {{ a.metrics.n }} returns whose supplier is known: the answer was hidden, the model guessed, and the guesses were compared with the truth ({{ a.metrics.cv_folds }}-fold cross-validation). The key question for the money is <b>how much of the returned value ends up on the wrong supplier</b>. Lower is better:</p>
{% set mx = ([a.metrics.misallocation] + (a.baselines.values() | map(attribute='misallocation') | list)) | max %}
<div class="bars">
  <div class="bar"><span><b>This model</b></span><div class="track"><div class="fill red" style="width: {{ (a.metrics.misallocation / mx * 100)|round(1) }}%"></div></div><span class="v">{{ a.metrics.misallocation | pct }}</span></div>
  {% for k, x in a.baselines.items() %}
  <div class="bar"><span>{{ x.label }}</span><div class="track"><div class="fill" style="width: {{ (x.misallocation / mx * 100)|round(1) }}%"></div></div><span class="v">{{ x.misallocation | pct }}</span></div>
  {% endfor %}
</div>
<p>So the model gets about <b>{{ (1 - a.metrics.misallocation) | pct(0) }} of the value right</b>, while every simple rule, including the one the assignment suggested, gets roughly half wrong. That rule (“blame whoever delivered that material most recently”) turned out not to match the returns where the answer is known.</p>
<div class="box note">
  <b>Be honest about the limits.</b> For a single return the model often can’t be sure, because several suppliers could have sent it and nothing on the return tells them apart. It picks the right supplier first {{ a.metrics.top1 | pct(0) }} of the time and has it in its top three {{ a.metrics.top3 | pct(0) }} of the time. That is why traced returns count towards the totals as an estimate, but <b>a supplier is only ever asked to pay for returns that are on record</b>.
</div>

<h2><span class="no">5</span>The scorecard</h2>
<p>Every supplier gets a score out of 100 (higher is better), built from four checks:</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Check</th><th class="n">Weight</th><th>What it measures</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Short delivery</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.short_delivery*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>How much of what was ordered never arrived</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Quality</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.quality_rejection*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>How much was rejected at our gate or returned by customers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Late delivery</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.late_delivery*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>How many days late orders arrive, on average</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Price</td><td class="n">{{ (s.weights.price_premium*100)|round|int }}%</td><td>How much more they charge than other suppliers for the same material</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A few rules keep the scores fair:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Compared with a typical supplier, not the worst.</b> Matching the typical supplier earns 100; only being clearly and meaningfully worse loses points. If nobody is bad, nobody is flagged. This was tested on data where every supplier was fine, and nobody was flagged.</li>
  <li><b>Small suppliers aren’t punished for bad luck.</b> A supplier with only a handful of orders is pulled towards the average until there’s enough evidence, so two bad orders can’t put them at the bottom.</li>
  <li><b>Price needs proof.</b> Prices vary a lot from order to order, so a supplier is only charged for high prices if a statistical test says it isn’t chance. {% if s.premium.significant %}That holds for {{ s.premium.significant | join(', ') }}.{% else %}No supplier passed, so ₹0 is claimed for price. The briefs still ask for a price match where a supplier charges above the others.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><b>Relationship length is ignored.</b> How long someone has supplied Arora says nothing about how well they deliver, so it isn’t used. A test proves changing it changes no score.</li>
  <li><b>The weights don’t decide the answer.</b> The same suppliers come out at the bottom under all {{ s.stability.scenarios | length }} different weightings tried, including equal weights.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="no">6</span>The double-check</h2>
{% if v.available and v.flagged %}
<p>With the ranking finished, the hidden “bad supplier” column was opened for the first time. It marks {{ v.flagged | join(', ') }}. The analysis, which never saw it, had placed exactly these suppliers at the bottom: ranks {% for k, r in v.flagged_ranks.items() %}{{ k }} #{{ r }}{{ ', ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %} of {{ v.n_suppliers }}, <b>{{ '%.0f'|format(v.gap_to_next) }} points clear</b> of the next supplier. That is a large, unambiguous gap, not a near miss.</p>
{% elif v.available %}
<p>The hidden answer column marks no supplier in this data, so there is nothing to check against; the ranking stands on the evidence alone.</p>
{% else %}
<p>This data has no hidden answer column, so the ranking stands on the evidence alone.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2><span class="no">7</span>Negotiation briefs and who to replace <span class="muted small">· 25% of marks</span></h2>
<p>Each of the {{ briefs | length }} worst suppliers gets a two-page A4 brief a buyer can take into a meeting:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Page one:</b> the rupee figure and how it was worked out, three or four numbered asks with the evidence for each, what the supplier is likely to argue back, and when to walk away.</li>
  <li><b>Page two:</b> the purchase orders to put on the table, and a breakdown by material.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every ask quotes a figure the supplier can check against their own invoices. For example, to {{ briefs[0].supplier_name }}:</p>
<div class="box"><i>“{{ briefs[0].asks[0].ask }}”</i><br><span class="muted small">Evidence: {{ briefs[0].asks[0].evidence }}</span></div>

<h3>Recommendation: replace these suppliers</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Replace</th><th>Main problems</th><th class="n">Cost</th><th>Who can take over their biggest materials</th></tr>
  {% for r in s.replacements %}
  <tr><td><b>{{ r.supplier_name }}</b></td><td>{% for d in r.drivers %}<span class="pill">{{ DIM.get(d, d) }}</span>{% endfor %}</td><td class="n">{{ r.total_impact | lakh }}</td>
    <td class="small">{% for m in r.materials[:3] %}{{ m.material }} → {{ m.alternatives[:2] | map(attribute='supplier_name') | join(', ') }}{{ '; ' if not loop.last }}{% endfor %}</td></tr>
  {% endfor %}
</table>
<p class="small muted">The alternatives are existing suppliers who already deliver that material well, so switching doesn’t mean finding someone new.</p>

<h2><span class="no">8</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Value of a return:</b> the supplier’s usual price for that material. <b>Handling a return:</b> assumed to cost {{ (cfg.RETURN_HANDLING_FACTOR*100)|round|int }}% of its value, and kept outside the assignment’s formula.</li>
  <li><b>Late-delivery penalty</b> in the briefs ({{ (cfg.LATE_PENALTY_PER_DAY*100)|round(1) }}% a day, capped at {{ (cfg.LATE_PENALTY_CAP*100)|round|int }}%) is a negotiating position, not a loss, and is never added to any total.</li>
  <li><b>Arora pays late too.</b> This is worked out from invoice dates and shown in each brief as the supplier’s likely comeback. It doesn’t change their score.</li>
  <li><b>Untraced returns are assumed to be like the traced ones.</b> If clerks tended to record some suppliers’ returns more than others’, the model would lean the wrong way, and the data can’t reveal that. This is the main reason only recorded returns are ever claimed. The lasting fix is simple: write a batch number on every return.</li>
  <li><b>Nothing is tuned to this dataset.</b> Suppliers, materials and thresholds are discovered from the data, and every analysis, including each upload, is worked out from its own files alone.</li>
</ul>

<h2><span class="no">9</span>How it was checked</h2>
<div class="two">
  <div>
    <p><b>An independent recount.</b> A separate script recalculates every headline figure straight from the raw files, without using any of the analysis code, and confirms the website shows the same numbers.</p>
    <p><b>Test data with a known answer.</b> Nine made-up datasets with planted bad suppliers: a 120-supplier panel, suppliers who turn bad only in the final year, a small but genuinely bad supplier, messy Indian spreadsheet exports, and one where nobody is bad. In all nine, the right suppliers were found, for the right reason, with no false alarms.</p>
  </div>
  <div>
    <p><b>Automatic tests</b> check the money formulas, that the hidden answer never reaches the scoring, that relationship length has no effect, the upload service, and every page of the website in a real browser.</p>
    <p><b>Try it.</b> The live website opens on these results. On its Upload page, anyone can add their own six files (any file names, Excel or Tally exports as they are) and get the same analysis for their suppliers.</p>
  </div>
</div>
